    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d903">

                <pb facs="0837" n="825" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg825"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">300.115(39) Manistique Paper and Pulp Co./2:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Germany</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Kirk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-10-19T18:00:00+01:00">October 19, 1939—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:49 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1762. Department’s 719, October 16, 7 p.m. The Consulate General at Hamburg
                    informed the Prize Commissioner of the ultimate destination to the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Minneapolis Tribune</hi> of the cargo of sulphite pulp
                    on board the detained Finnish vessel <hi rendition="#italic">Margareta</hi>. The
                    Commissioner stated that nothing short of an official Government guarantee of
                    non-reexportation would be acceptable to effect release. (See telegram 163 from
                    the Consulate General at Hamburg.<anchor n="98" xml:id="d903-f1"/> There appears
                    to be no other grounds at present for the detention.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
